+# Hidden contents and attachment policy
+
+A newly created bridge tab is hidden. Native attachment and the host's real
+presentation intent, combined with AppKit visibility/occlusion, determine
+WebContents visibility. A tab that has never been attached is not evidence of
+an imminent foreground attachment. There is no implicit scheduler exemption
+for an "attached-pending" state.
+
+Hidden means Chromium's normal background policy, not freeze, discard, mute,
+stop or process-wide parking. In particular, a script evaluation can start
+promptly while the Promise it returns waits for a throttled timer or a frame
+that a hidden document does not produce. Do not make an unattached WebContents
+visible to accelerate such a Promise.
+
+## Wave 16 measurement
+
+macOS arm64 component/debug build, Chromium 156.0.8078.74, ABI 1.13. Four fresh
+processes each alternate six hidden and six attached loopback loads, then issue
+one cold isolated evaluation, a warm literal evaluation, eight sequential 5 ms
+timers, and requestAnimationFrame. A disposable mock keychain removes OS key
+authorization from this experiment. The document reports hidden/visible as
+expected in all 48 visibility queries. No production flag is changed.
+
+Medians in milliseconds, hidden / attached (six of each per control):
+
+| Diagnostic control | Warm `42` | Eight chained timers |
+|---|---:|---:|
+| Default | 4.112 / 6.060 | 10670.734 / 54.915 |
+| Background timer throttling + intensive feature disabled | 3.845 / 4.172 | 53.506 / 54.578 |
+| Renderer backgrounding disabled | 3.686 / 4.637 | 7323.323 / 52.793 |
+| Frame-rate cap disabled | 4.312 / 6.380 | 7450.831 / 54.096 |
+
+Cold isolated evaluation includes CDP/world setup: default medians are
+45.651 / 64.089 ms, hidden maximum 198.132 ms. The default hidden timer chain
+has a 26240.084 ms maximum; its callback remains within the ABI's 30-second
+bound. A pilot's 12-second assertion was too short to measure this tail and
+was replaced with explicit timeout recording, rather than a production change.
+
+All 24 hidden requestAnimationFrame Promises hit their 350 ms evaluation
+deadline; all 24 attached cases complete (per-control medians 5.242–6.362 ms).
+Removing the frame-rate cap does not grant hidden pages animation frames.
+Removing timer throttling changes timer latency while visibility stays hidden.
+This identifies timer-dependent evaluation slowness; immediate evaluation is
+not generally held for seconds by hidden state in this experiment.
+
+Fresh documents here are younger than the intensive throttling grace periods
+at this pin (60 seconds loaded / 5 minutes loading). This is evidence for the
+ordinary wake-up/budget policy, not a reproduction of aged intensive throttling.
+
+Load-only measurements are recorded separately to avoid contaminating the next
+tab's startup interval with the preceding evaluation's long timer wait.
+Ten hidden and ten attached first loads per control (80 loads, zero 20-second
+watchdog timeouts) give:
+
+| Diagnostic control | Hidden p50 / max (ms) | Attached p50 / max (ms) |
+|---|---:|---:|
+| Default | 1277.564 / 3993.049 | 1261.942 / 3403.571 |
+| Timer throttling disabled | 1268.745 / 4021.423 | 1265.106 / 2678.631 |
+| Renderer backgrounding disabled | 1305.996 / 3753.998 | 1259.717 / 2509.024 |
+| Frame-rate cap disabled | 1288.858 / 3365.803 | 1509.423 / 5624.783 |
+
+The controls show no first-load improvement that justifies a policy change.
+Cold component/debug renderer startup remains variable, including seconds-long
+samples in both visibility states. A median from one fresh process per control
+is diagnostic evidence, not an optimized-build performance guarantee.
+
+## Host guidance
+
+Preserve the visibility/attachment contract. Attach and present an intended
+foreground tab when its real native host is available. Do not await rAF before
+attachment, and avoid timer-based polling to bootstrap hidden editor code;
+use direct evaluation for explicit snapshots and the isolated message channel
+for document/input events. An engine-wide exemption inferred from "never
+attached" would also exempt genuinely hidden background tabs. These findings
+do not justify that policy change or a global timer/frame/priority switch.
+Any future preparation capability needs explicit bounded host intent and a
+separate contract; ABI 1.13 adds no such capability.
